{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22242","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22242","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22242","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiahui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yurui","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yurui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yueming","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yueming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ze","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Ze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanpeng","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yanpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Jie","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Yu-Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyue","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Xinyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guowei","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Guowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingyue","familyName":"Quan","name":"Quan, Xingyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"4D-VLA: Spatiotemporal Vision-Language-Action Pretraining with Cross-Scene Calibration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T14:09:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:39:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-18T14:43:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-19T01:53:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Leveraging diverse robotic data for pretraining remains a critical challenge. Existing methods typically model the dataset's action distribution using simple observations as inputs. However, these inputs are often incomplete, resulting in a dispersed conditional action distribution-an issue we refer to as coordinate system chaos and state chaos. This inconsistency significantly hampers pretraining efficiency. To address this, we propose 4D-VLA, a novel approach that effectively integrates 4D information into the input to mitigate these sources of chaos. Our model introduces depth and temporal information into visual features with sequential RGB-D inputs, aligning the coordinate systems of the robot and the scene. This alignment endows the model with strong spatiotemporal reasoning capabilities while minimizing training overhead. Additionally, we introduce memory bank sampling, a frame sampling strategy designed to extract informative frames from historical images, further improving effectiveness and efficiency. Experimental results demonstrate that our pretraining method and architectural components substantially enhance model performance. In both simulated and real-world experiments, our model achieves a significant increase in success rate over OpenVLA. To further assess spatial perception and generalization to novel views, we introduce MV-Bench, a multi-view simulation benchmark. Our model consistently outperforms existing methods, demonstrating stronger spatial understanding and adaptability."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22242","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:37:41Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:37:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-19T03:14:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22432","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22432","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22432","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yuhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tengfei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Tengfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Fang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenwei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhenwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rynson W. H.","familyName":"Lau","name":"Lau, Rynson W. H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Shape-for-Motion: Precise and Consistent Video Editing with 3D Proxy"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T17:59:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:50:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-26T06:29:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-29T00:36:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in deep generative modeling have unlocked unprecedented opportunities for video synthesis. In real-world applications, however, users often seek tools to faithfully realize their creative editing intentions with precise and consistent control. Despite the progress achieved by existing methods, ensuring fine-grained alignment with user intentions remains an open and challenging problem. In this work, we present Shape-for-Motion, a novel framework that incorporates a 3D proxy for precise and consistent video editing. Shape-for-Motion achieves this by converting the target object in the input video to a time-consistent mesh, i.e., a 3D proxy, allowing edits to be performed directly on the proxy and then inferred back to the video frames. To simplify the editing process, we design a novel Dual-Propagation Strategy that allows users to perform edits on the 3D mesh of a single frame, and the edits are then automatically propagated to the 3D meshes of the other frames. The 3D meshes for different frames are further projected onto the 2D space to produce the edited geometry and texture renderings, which serve as inputs to a decoupled video diffusion model for generating edited results. Our framework supports various precise and physically-consistent manipulations across the video frames, including pose editing, rotation, scaling, translation, texture modification, and object composition. Our approach marks a key step toward high-quality, controllable video editing workflows. Extensive experiments demonstrate the superiority and effectiveness of our approach. Project page: https://shapeformotion.github.io/"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by Siggraph Asia 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22432","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:42:10Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:42:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-29T02:53:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22267","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22267","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22267","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junaid Ahmed","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Junaid Ahmed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hiari Pizzini","familyName":"Cavagna","name":"Cavagna, Hiari Pizzini","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrea","familyName":"Proia","name":"Proia, Andrea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrea","familyName":"Bartolini","name":"Bartolini, Andrea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Data Center IoT Telemetry to Data Analytics Chatbots -- Virtual Knowledge Graph is All You Need"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T14:36:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:41:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-14T11:40:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-08-15T00:33:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Industry 5.0 demands IoT systems that support seamless human-machine collaboration, yet current IoT data analysis requires deep domain, deployment, and query expertise. We show that combining Large Language Models (LLMs) with Knowledge Graphs (KGs) enables natural language access to heterogeneous IoT data. Focusing on data center IoT telemetry, we introduce a rule-based Virtual Knowledge Graph (VKG) construction process and an on-premise LLM inference service to create an end-to-end Data Analytics (DA) chatbot. Our system dynamically generates VKGs per query and translates user input into SPARQL, achieving 92.5% accuracy (vs. 25% for LLM-to-NoSQL) while reducing latency by 85% (20.36s to 3.03s) and keeping VKG sizes under 179 MiB. This work demonstrates that VKG-powered LLM interfaces deliver accurate, low-latency, and relationship-aware access to large-scale telemetry, bridging the gap between users and complex IoT systems in Industry 5.0."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22267","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:38:17Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:38:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-08-15T01:54:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22355","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22355","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22355","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pascale","familyName":"Fung","name":"Fung, Pascale","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoram","familyName":"Bachrach","name":"Bachrach, Yoram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Asli","familyName":"Celikyilmaz","name":"Celikyilmaz, Asli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kamalika","familyName":"Chaudhuri","name":"Chaudhuri, Kamalika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Delong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Delong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Willy","familyName":"Chung","name":"Chung, Willy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emmanuel","familyName":"Dupoux","name":"Dupoux, Emmanuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyu","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Hongyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hervé","familyName":"Jégou","name":"Jégou, Hervé","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessandro","familyName":"Lazaric","name":"Lazaric, Alessandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arjun","familyName":"Majumdar","name":"Majumdar, Arjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrea","familyName":"Madotto","name":"Madotto, Andrea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Franziska","familyName":"Meier","name":"Meier, Franziska","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Florian","familyName":"Metze","name":"Metze, Florian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Louis-Philippe","familyName":"Morency","name":"Morency, Louis-Philippe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Théo","familyName":"Moutakanni","name":"Moutakanni, Théo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juan","familyName":"Pino","name":"Pino, Juan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Basile","familyName":"Terver","name":"Terver, Basile","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joseph","familyName":"Tighe","name":"Tighe, Joseph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paden","familyName":"Tomasello","name":"Tomasello, Paden","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jitendra","familyName":"Malik","name":"Malik, Jitendra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Embodied AI Agents: Modeling the World"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T16:05:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:45:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-03T11:47:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-04T00:33:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-07T15:42:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-07-08T02:02:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper describes our research on AI agents embodied in visual, virtual or physical forms, enabling them to interact with both users and their environments. These agents, which include virtual avatars, wearable devices, and robots, are designed to perceive, learn and act within their surroundings, which makes them more similar to how humans learn and interact with the environments as compared to disembodied agents. We propose that the development of world models is central to reasoning and planning of embodied AI agents, allowing these agents to understand and predict their environment, to understand user intentions and social contexts, thereby enhancing their ability to perform complex tasks autonomously. World modeling encompasses the integration of multimodal perception, planning through reasoning for action and control, and memory to create a comprehensive understanding of the physical world. Beyond the physical world, we also propose to learn the mental world model of users to enable better human-agent collaboration."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22355","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:40:22Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:40:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-08T04:32:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22735","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22735","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22735","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Willem","familyName":"Conradie","name":"Conradie, Willem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Krishna","familyName":"Manoorkar","name":"Manoorkar, Krishna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessandra","familyName":"Palmigiano","name":"Palmigiano, Alessandra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Apostolos","familyName":"Tzimoulis","name":"Tzimoulis, Apostolos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nachoem","familyName":"Wijnberg","name":"Wijnberg, Nachoem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Questions as cognitive filters"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-28T02:43:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T00:12:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we develop a logico-algebraic framework for modeling decision-making through deliberation in multi-agent settings. The central concept in this framework is that of interrogative agendas, which represent the cognitive stances of agents regarding which features should be considered relevant in the final decision. We formalize an agent's interrogative agenda as an equivalence relation that identifies outcomes differing only in aspects the agent deems irrelevant. Moreover, we characterize the sublattices of the resulting lattice that correspond to relevant interrogative agendas for deliberation scenarios governed by different ``winning rules.\" We then introduce a two-sorted logico-algebraic structure-comprising the lattice of relevant interrogative agendas and the Boolean algebras of agent coalitions-to model the interaction between agents and agendas during deliberation. Finally, we discuss which interaction conditions can and cannot be defined within this framework."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22735","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:29:19Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:29:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:29:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22708","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22708","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22708","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shrenik","familyName":"Jadhav","name":"Jadhav, Shrenik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Birva","familyName":"Sevak","name":"Sevak, Birva","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Srijita","familyName":"Das","name":"Das, Srijita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akhtar","familyName":"Hussain","name":"Hussain, Akhtar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wencong","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Wencong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Van-Hai","familyName":"Bui","name":"Bui, Van-Hai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FairMarket-RL: LLM-Guided Fairness Shaping for Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning in Peer-to-Peer Markets"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"General Economics (econ.GN)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-28T01:17:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T00:11:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Peer-to-peer (P2P) trading is increasingly recognized as a key mechanism for decentralized market regulation, yet existing approaches often lack robust frameworks to ensure fairness. This paper presents FairMarket-RL, a novel hybrid framework that combines Large Language Models (LLMs) with Reinforcement Learning (RL) to enable fairness-aware trading agents. In a simulated P2P microgrid with multiple sellers and buyers, the LLM acts as a real-time fairness critic, evaluating each trading episode using two metrics: Fairness-To-Buyer (FTB) and Fairness-Between-Sellers (FBS). These fairness scores are integrated into agent rewards through scheduled λ-coefficients, forming an adaptive LLM-guided reward shaping loop that replaces brittle, rule-based fairness constraints. Agents are trained using Independent Proximal Policy Optimization (IPPO) and achieve equitable outcomes, fulfilling over 90% of buyer demand, maintaining fair seller margins, and consistently reaching FTB and FBS scores above 0.80. The training process demonstrates that fairness feedback improves convergence, reduces buyer shortfalls, and narrows profit disparities between sellers. With its language-based critic, the framework scales naturally, and its extension to a large power distribution system with household prosumers illustrates its practical applicability. FairMarket-RL thus offers a scalable, equity-driven solution for autonomous trading in decentralized energy systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22708","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:28:40Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:28:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:28:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22706","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22706","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22706","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arun","familyName":"Ramamurthy","name":"Ramamurthy, Arun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Neil","familyName":"Dhir","name":"Dhir, Neil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"General Autonomous Cybersecurity Defense: Learning Robust Policies for Dynamic Topologies and Diverse Attackers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-28T01:12:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T00:10:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In the face of evolving cyber threats such as malware, ransomware and phishing, autonomous cybersecurity defense (ACD) systems have become essential for real-time threat detection and response with optional human intervention. However, existing ACD systems rely on limiting assumptions, particularly the stationarity of the underlying network dynamics. In real-world scenarios, network topologies can change due to actions taken by attackers or defenders, system failures, or time evolution of networks, leading to failures in the adaptive capabilities of current defense agents. Moreover, many agents are trained on static environments, resulting in overfitting to specific topologies, which hampers their ability to generalize to out-of-distribution network topologies. This work addresses these challenges by exploring methods for developing agents to learn generalizable policies across dynamic network environments -- general ACD (GACD)."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22706","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:28:38Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:28:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:28:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22703","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22703","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22703","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wali Mohammad","familyName":"Abdullah","name":"Abdullah, Wali Mohammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Azmain","familyName":"Kabir","name":"Kabir, Azmain","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"P4OMP: Retrieval-Augmented Prompting for OpenMP Parallelism in Serial Code"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-28T01:06:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T00:10:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present P4OMP, a retrieval-augmented framework for transforming serial C/C++ code into OpenMP-annotated parallel code using large language models (LLMs). To our knowledge, this is the first system to apply retrieval-based prompting for OpenMP pragma correctness without model fine-tuning or compiler instrumentation. P4OMP leverages Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) with structured instructional knowledge from OpenMP tutorials to improve the reliability of prompt-driven code generation. By grounding generation in the retrieved context, P4OMP improves syntactic correctness compared to baseline prompting with GPT-3.5-Turbo. We evaluate P4OMP against a baseline, GPT-3.5-Turbo without retrieval, on a comprehensive benchmark of 108 real-world C++ programs drawn from Stack Overflow, PolyBench, and NAS benchmark suites. P4OMP achieves 100% compilation success on all parallelizable cases, while the baseline fails to compile in 20 out of 108 cases. Six cases that rely on non-random-access iterators or thread-unsafe constructs are excluded due to fundamental OpenMP limitations. A detailed analysis demonstrates how P4OMP consistently avoids scoping errors, syntactic misuse, and invalid directive combinations that commonly affect baseline-generated code. We further demonstrate strong runtime scaling across seven compute-intensive benchmarks on an HPC cluster. P4OMP offers a robust, modular pipeline that significantly improves the reliability and applicability of LLM-generated OpenMP code."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22703","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:28:34Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:28:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:28:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22696","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22696","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22696","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brian","familyName":"Mak","name":"Mak, Brian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeffrey","familyName":"Flanigan","name":"Flanigan, Jeffrey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Residual Matrix Transformers: Scaling the Size of the Residual Stream"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-28T00:29:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T00:10:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The residual stream acts as a memory bus where transformer layers both store and access features (Elhage et al., 2021). We consider changing the mechanism for retrieving and storing information in the residual stream, and replace the residual stream of the transformer with an outer product memory matrix (Kohonen, 1972, Anderson, 1972). We call this model the Residual Matrix Transformer (RMT). We find that the RMT enjoys a number of attractive properties: 1) the size of the residual stream can be scaled independently of compute and model size, improving performance, 2) the RMT can achieve the same loss as the transformer with 58% fewer FLOPS, 25% fewer parameters, and 41% fewer training tokens tokens, and 3) the RMT outperforms the transformer on downstream evaluations. We theoretically analyze the transformer and the RMT, and show that the RMT allows for more efficient scaling of the residual stream, as well as improved variance propagation properties. Code for this project can be found at https://github.com/bmac3/residual-matrix-transformer."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICML 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22696","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:28:24Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:28:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:28:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22656","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22656","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22656","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiangping","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jiangping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongming","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Dongming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weisong","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Weisong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhi","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Zhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Knowledge-Guided Multi-Agent Framework for Automated Requirements Development: A Vision"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"D.2.3; I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68-04","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T21:57:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T00:08:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper envisions a knowledge-guided multi-agent framework named KGMAF for automated requirements development. KGMAF aims to address gaps in current automation systems for SE, which prioritize code development and overlook the complexities of requirements tasks. KGMAF is composed of six specialized agents and an artifact pool to improve efficiency and accuracy. Specifically, KGMAF outlines the functionality, actions, and knowledge of each agent and provides the conceptual design of the artifact pool. Our case study highlights the potential of KGMAF in real-world scenarios. Finally, we outline several research opportunities for implementing and enhancing automated requirements development using multi-agent systems. We believe that KGMAF will play a pivotal role in shaping the future of automated requirements development in the era of LLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22656","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:27:26Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:27:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:27:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22644","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22644","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22644","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chase","familyName":"Fensore","name":"Fensore, Chase","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaustubh","familyName":"Dhole","name":"Dhole, Kaustubh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joyce C","familyName":"Ho","name":"Ho, Joyce C","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eugene","familyName":"Agichtein","name":"Agichtein, Eugene","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating Hybrid Retrieval Augmented Generation using Dynamic Test Sets: LiveRAG Challenge"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T21:20:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T00:07:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present our submission to the LiveRAG Challenge 2025, which evaluates retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) systems on dynamic test sets using the FineWeb-10BT corpus. Our final hybrid approach combines sparse (BM25) and dense (E5) retrieval methods and then aims to generate relevant and faithful answers with Falcon3-10B-Instruct. Through systematic evaluation on 200 synthetic questions generated with DataMorgana across 64 unique question-user combinations, we demonstrate that neural re-ranking with RankLLaMA improves MAP from 0.523 to 0.797 (52% relative improvement) but introduces prohibitive computational costs (84s vs 1.74s per question). While DSPy-optimized prompting strategies achieved higher semantic similarity (0.771 vs 0.668), their 0% refusal rates raised concerns about over-confidence and generalizability. Our submitted hybrid system without re-ranking achieved 4th place in faithfulness and 11th place in correctness among 25 teams. Analysis across question categories reveals that vocabulary alignment between questions and documents was the strongest predictor of performance on our development set, with document-similar phrasing improving cosine similarity from 0.562 to 0.762."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"4 pages, 3 tables, 2 figures. Accepted at the SIGIR LiveRAG Workshop 2025 (Submission 2664)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22644","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:27:09Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:27:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:27:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22636","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22636","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22636","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sotirios Panagiotis","familyName":"Chytas","name":"Chytas, Sotirios Panagiotis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miso","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Miso","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyunwoo J.","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Hyunwoo J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vikas","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Vikas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ReCo: Reminder Composition Mitigates Hallucinations in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T21:01:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T00:07:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision Language Models (VLMs) show impressive capabilities in integrating and reasoning with both visual and language data. But these models make mistakes. A common finding -- similar to LLMs -- is their tendency to hallucinate, i.e., generate plausible sounding text which is not grounded in the visual input, or at worst, is contradictory. A growing consensus attributes this behavior to an over-reliance on language -- especially as the generation progresses, the model suffers from a ``fading memory effect'' with respect to the provided visual input. We study mechanisms by which this behavior can be controlled. Specifically, using ideas from geometric algebra and relational compositions, we propose the addition of a small, trainable module (named ReCo) on top of any VLM -- no other modification is needed. We show that such a lightweight module is able to mitigate the fading memory effect on three of the most widely used VLMs (InstructBLIP, LlaVA, MiniGPT4), where we see performance improvements on multiple benchmarks. Additionally, we show that our module can be combined with many of the other approaches for reducing hallucination where we achieve improved results for each one."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22636","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:26:58Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:26:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:26:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22591","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22591","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22591","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arunkumar","familyName":"Kannan","name":"Kannan, Arunkumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin A.","familyName":"Lindquist","name":"Lindquist, Martin A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brian","familyName":"Caffo","name":"Caffo, Brian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BrainMT: A Hybrid Mamba-Transformer Architecture for Modeling Long-Range Dependencies in Functional MRI Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T19:20:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T00:05:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in deep learning have made it possible to predict phenotypic measures directly from functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) brain volumes, sparking significant interest in the neuroimaging community. However, existing approaches, primarily based on convolutional neural networks or transformer architectures, often struggle to model the complex relationships inherent in fMRI data, limited by their inability to capture long-range spatial and temporal dependencies. To overcome these shortcomings, we introduce BrainMT, a novel hybrid framework designed to efficiently learn and integrate long-range spatiotemporal attributes in fMRI data. Our framework operates in two stages: (1) a bidirectional Mamba block with a temporal-first scanning mechanism to capture global temporal interactions in a computationally efficient manner; and (2) a transformer block leveraging self-attention to model global spatial relationships across the deep features processed by the Mamba block. Extensive experiments on two large-scale public datasets, UKBioBank and the Human Connectome Project, demonstrate that BrainMT achieves state-of-the-art performance on both classification (sex prediction) and regression (cognitive intelligence prediction) tasks, outperforming existing methods by a significant margin. Our code and implementation details will be made publicly available at this https://github.com/arunkumar-kannan/BrainMT-fMRI"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at MICCAI 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22591","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:25:53Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:25:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:25:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22396","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22396","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22396","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danush","familyName":"Khanna","name":"Khanna, Danush","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aditya Kumar","familyName":"Guru","name":"Guru, Aditya Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Srivarshinee","familyName":"Sridhar","name":"Sridhar, Srivarshinee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zidan","familyName":"Ahmed","name":"Ahmed, Zidan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rubhav","familyName":"Bahirwani","name":"Bahirwani, Rubhav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meetu","familyName":"Malhotra","name":"Malhotra, Meetu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vinija","familyName":"Jain","name":"Jain, Vinija","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aman","familyName":"Chadha","name":"Chadha, Aman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amitava","familyName":"Das","name":"Das, Amitava","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kripabandhu","familyName":"Ghosh","name":"Ghosh, Kripabandhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"QuickSilver -- Speeding up LLM Inference through Dynamic Token Halting, KV Skipping, Contextual Token Fusion, and Adaptive Matryoshka Quantization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.0; I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T17:10:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:48:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Inference accounts for the majority of latency and energy consumption in large language model (LLM) deployments, often exceeding 90% of total cost. While training-time efficiency has seen extensive progress, runtime optimization remains a key bottleneck, particularly under autoregressive decoding. Existing approaches -- such as pruning, quantization, early exits, and speculative decoding -- often require retraining, architectural changes, or disrupt decoding compatibility. We introduce QuickSilver, a modular, token-level framework that enables semantic adaptivity at inference time without altering model weights or structure. QuickSilver integrates four synergistic mechanisms:\n (i) Dynamic Token Halting, which halts computation for tokens with converged representations; (ii) KV Cache Skipping, which selectively suppresses memory writes to reduce attention overhead; and (iii) Contextual Token Fusion, which collapses redundant tokens into shared paths to shrink sequence length.\n Unlike speculative decoding or MoE routing, QuickSilver operates entirely on frozen, dense models and requires no auxiliary networks. Applied to GPT-2 and Llama-2 across WikiText-103 and C4, QuickSilver achieves up to 39.6% FLOP reduction with negligible perplexity degradation (\u0026lt;=0.2)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint. Under submission"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22396","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:41:20Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:41:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:41:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22395","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22395","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22395","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shih-Han","familyName":"Chou","name":"Chou, Shih-Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shivam","familyName":"Chandhok","name":"Chandhok, Shivam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James J.","familyName":"Little","name":"Little, James J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leonid","familyName":"Sigal","name":"Sigal, Leonid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Test-Time Consistency in Vision Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T17:09:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:48:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs) have achieved impressive performance across a wide range of multimodal tasks, yet they often exhibit inconsistent behavior when faced with semantically equivalent inputs, undermining their reliability and robustness. Recent benchmarks, such as MM-R3, highlight that even state-of-the-art VLMs can produce divergent predictions across semantically equivalent inputs, despite maintaining high average accuracy. Prior work addresses this issue by modifying model architectures or conducting large-scale fine-tuning on curated datasets. In contrast, we propose a simple and effective test-time consistency framework that enhances semantic consistency without supervised re-training. Our method is entirely post-hoc, model-agnostic, and applicable to any VLM with access to its weights. Given a single test point, we enforce consistent predictions via two complementary objectives: (i) a Cross-Entropy Agreement Loss that aligns predictive distributions across semantically equivalent inputs, and (ii) a Pseudo-Label Consistency Loss that draws outputs toward a self-averaged consensus. Our method is plug-and-play and leverages information from a single test input itself to improve consistency. Experiments on the MM-R3 benchmark show that our framework yields substantial gains in consistency across state-of-the-art models, establishing a new direction for inference-time adaptation in multimodal learning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22395","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:41:18Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:41:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:41:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22375","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22375","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22375","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tiankai","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Tiankai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yushu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yushu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"Goodge","name":"Goodge, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Teng","name":"Teng, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xulei","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xulei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianrui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Tianrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xun","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exploiting Vision Language Model for Training-Free 3D Point Cloud OOD Detection via Graph Score Propagation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T16:42:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:47:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Out-of-distribution (OOD) detection in 3D point cloud data remains a challenge, particularly in applications where safe and robust perception is critical. While existing OOD detection methods have shown progress for 2D image data, extending these to 3D environments involves unique obstacles. This paper introduces a training-free framework that leverages Vision-Language Models (VLMs) for effective OOD detection in 3D point clouds. By constructing a graph based on class prototypes and testing data, we exploit the data manifold structure to enhancing the effectiveness of VLMs for 3D OOD detection. We propose a novel Graph Score Propagation (GSP) method that incorporates prompt clustering and self-training negative prompting to improve OOD scoring with VLM. Our method is also adaptable to few-shot scenarios, providing options for practical applications. We demonstrate that GSP consistently outperforms state-of-the-art methods across synthetic and real-world datasets 3D point cloud OOD detection."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICCV 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22375","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:40:50Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:40:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:40:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22366","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22366","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22366","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daichi","familyName":"Kato","name":"Kato, Daichi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryo","familyName":"Ueda","name":"Ueda, Ryo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yusuke","familyName":"Miyao","name":"Miyao, Yusuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Why Are Parsing Actions for Understanding Message Hierarchies Not Random?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T16:27:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:46:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"If humans understood language by randomly selecting parsing actions, it might have been necessary to construct a robust symbolic system capable of being interpreted under any hierarchical structure. However, human parsing strategies do not seem to follow such a random pattern. Why is that the case? In fact, a previous study on emergent communication using models with hierarchical biases have reported that agents adopting random parsing strategies$\\unicode{x2013}$ones that deviate significantly from human language comprehension$\\unicode{x2013}$can achieve high communication accuracy. In this study, we investigate this issue by making two simple and natural modifications to the experimental setup: (I) we use more complex inputs that have hierarchical structures, such that random parsing makes semantic interpretation more difficult, and (II) we incorporate a surprisal-related term, which is known to influence the order of words and characters in natural language, into the objective function. With these changes, we evaluate whether agents employing random parsing strategies still maintain high communication accuracy."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22366","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:40:37Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:40:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:40:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22365","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22365","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22365","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haozhe","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Haozhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingsheng","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Mingsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaqi","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yaqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reinforcement Learning with Physics-Informed Symbolic Program Priors for Zero-Shot Wireless Indoor Navigation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T16:26:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:46:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"When using reinforcement learning (RL) to tackle physical control tasks, inductive biases that encode physics priors can help improve sample efficiency during training and enhance generalization in testing. However, the current practice of incorporating these helpful physics-informed inductive biases inevitably runs into significant manual labor and domain expertise, making them prohibitive for general users. This work explores a symbolic approach to distill physics-informed inductive biases into RL agents, where the physics priors are expressed in a domain-specific language (DSL) that is human-readable and naturally explainable. Yet, the DSL priors do not translate directly into an implementable policy due to partial and noisy observations and additional physical constraints in navigation tasks. To address this gap, we develop a physics-informed program-guided RL (PiPRL) framework with applications to indoor navigation. PiPRL adopts a hierarchical and modularized neuro-symbolic integration, where a meta symbolic program receives semantically meaningful features from a neural perception module, which form the bases for symbolic programming that encodes physics priors and guides the RL process of a low-level neural controller. Extensive experiments demonstrate that PiPRL consistently outperforms purely symbolic or neural policies and reduces training time by over 26% with the help of the program-based inductive biases."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Spotlight paper at Reinforcement Learning Conference 2025, Workshop on Inductive Biases in Reinforcement Learning"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22365","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:40:36Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:40:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:40:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22362","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22362","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22362","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunpeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yunpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"George","familyName":"Sung","name":"Sung, George","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shao-Fu","familyName":"Shih","name":"Shih, Shao-Fu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Craig","familyName":"Dooley","name":"Dooley, Craig","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessio","familyName":"Centazzo","name":"Centazzo, Alessio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ramanan","familyName":"Rajeswaran","name":"Rajeswaran, Ramanan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DiffSoundStream: Efficient Speech Tokenization via Diffusion Decoding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T16:23:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:46:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Token-based language modeling is a prominent approach for speech generation, where tokens are obtained by quantizing features from self-supervised learning (SSL) models and extracting codes from neural speech codecs, generally referred to as semantic tokens and acoustic tokens. These tokens are often modeled autoregressively, with the inference speed being constrained by the token rate. In this work, we propose DiffSoundStream, a solution that improves the efficiency of speech tokenization in non-streaming scenarios through two techniques: (1) conditioning the neural codec on semantic tokens to minimize redundancy between semantic and acoustic tokens, and (2) leveraging latent diffusion models to synthesize high-quality waveforms from semantic and coarse-level acoustic tokens. Experiments show that at 50 tokens per second, DiffSoundStream achieves speech quality on par with a standard SoundStream model operating at twice the token rate. Additionally, we achieve step-size distillation using just four diffusion sampling steps with only a minor quality loss."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22362","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:40:32Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:40:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:40:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22305","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22305","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22305","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Albert Agisha","familyName":"Ntwali","name":"Ntwali, Albert Agisha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Rück","name":"Rück, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Heckmann","name":"Heckmann, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Detection of Personal Data in Structured Datasets Using a Large Language Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.5.4; I.2.7; H.3.1","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T15:16:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:42:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose a novel approach for detecting personal data in structured datasets, leveraging GPT-4o, a state-of-the-art Large Language Model. A key innovation of our method is the incorporation of contextual information: in addition to a feature's name and values, we utilize information from other feature names within the dataset as well as the dataset description. We compare our approach to alternative methods, including Microsoft Presidio and CASSED, evaluating them on multiple datasets: DeSSI, a large synthetic dataset, datasets we collected from Kaggle and OpenML as well as MIMIC-Demo-Ext, a real-world dataset containing patient information from critical care units.\n Our findings reveal that detection performance varies significantly depending on the dataset used for evaluation. CASSED excels on DeSSI, the dataset on which it was trained. Performance on the medical dataset MIMIC-Demo-Ext is comparable across all models, with our GPT-4o-based approach clearly outperforming the others. Notably, personal data detection in the Kaggle and OpenML datasets appears to benefit from contextual information. This is evidenced by the poor performance of CASSED and Presidio (both of which do not utilize the context of the dataset) compared to the strong results of our GPT-4o-based approach.\n We conclude that further progress in this field would greatly benefit from the availability of more real-world datasets containing personal information."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22305","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:39:10Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:39:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:39:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22298","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22298","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22298","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linhao","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Linhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Fan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianzhuang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jianzhuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renjing","familyName":"Pei","name":"Pei, Renjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fenglong","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Fenglong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OutDreamer: Video Outpainting with a Diffusion Transformer"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T15:08:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:42:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Video outpainting is a challenging task that generates new video content by extending beyond the boundaries of an original input video, requiring both temporal and spatial consistency. Many state-of-the-art methods utilize latent diffusion models with U-Net backbones but still struggle to achieve high quality and adaptability in generated content. Diffusion transformers (DiTs) have emerged as a promising alternative because of their superior performance. We introduce OutDreamer, a DiT-based video outpainting framework comprising two main components: an efficient video control branch and a conditional outpainting branch. The efficient video control branch effectively extracts masked video information, while the conditional outpainting branch generates missing content based on these extracted conditions. Additionally, we propose a mask-driven self-attention layer that dynamically integrates the given mask information, further enhancing the model's adaptability to outpainting tasks. Furthermore, we introduce a latent alignment loss to maintain overall consistency both within and between frames. For long video outpainting, we employ a cross-video-clip refiner to iteratively generate missing content, ensuring temporal consistency across video clips. Extensive evaluations demonstrate that our zero-shot OutDreamer outperforms state-of-the-art zero-shot methods on widely recognized benchmarks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22298","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:39:00Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:39:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:39:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22276","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22276","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22276","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reuth","familyName":"Mirsky","name":"Mirsky, Reuth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Artificial Intelligent Disobedience: Rethinking the Agency of Our Artificial Teammates"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T14:45:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:41:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Artificial intelligence has made remarkable strides in recent years, achieving superhuman performance across a wide range of tasks. Yet despite these advances, most cooperative AI systems remain rigidly obedient, designed to follow human instructions without question and conform to user expectations, even when doing so may be counterproductive or unsafe. This paper argues for expanding the agency of AI teammates to include \\textit{intelligent disobedience}, empowering them to make meaningful and autonomous contributions within human-AI teams. It introduces a scale of AI agency levels and uses representative examples to highlight the importance and growing necessity of treating AI autonomy as an independent research focus in cooperative settings. The paper then explores how intelligent disobedience manifests across different autonomy levels and concludes by proposing initial boundaries and considerations for studying disobedience as a core capability of artificial agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Extended version of a paper accepted for publication in AI Magazine"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22276","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:38:29Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:38:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:38:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22262","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22262","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22262","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Evgeny","familyName":"Dedov","name":"Dedov, Evgeny","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"JointRank: Rank Large Set with Single Pass"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"H.3.3","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T14:30:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:40:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3731120.3744587","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Efficiently ranking relevant items from large candidate pools is a cornerstone of modern information retrieval systems -- such as web search, recommendation, and retrieval-augmented generation. Listwise rerankers, which improve relevance by jointly considering multiple candidates, are often limited in practice: either by model input size constraints, or by degraded quality when processing large sets. We propose a model-agnostic method for fast reranking large sets that exceed a model input limits. The method first partitions candidate items into overlapping blocks, each of which is ranked independently in parallel. Implicit pairwise comparisons are then derived from these local rankings. Finally, these comparisons are aggregated to construct a global ranking using algorithms such as Winrate or PageRank. Experiments on TREC DL-2019 show that our method achieves an nDCG@10 of 70.88 compared to the 57.68 for full-context listwise approach using gpt-4.1-mini as long-context model, while reducing latency from 21 to 8 seconds.\n The implementation of the algorithm and the experiments is available in the repository: https://github.com/V3RGANz/jointrank"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICTIR'25 Accepted"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22262","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-06-30T01:38:10Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:38:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:38:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22255","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22255","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22255","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maciej","familyName":"Stefaniak","name":"Stefaniak, Maciej","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michał","familyName":"Krutul","name":"Krutul, Michał","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan","familyName":"Małaśnicki","name":"Małaśnicki, Jan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maciej","familyName":"Pióro","name":"Pióro, Maciej","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jakub","familyName":"Krajewski","name":"Krajewski, Jakub","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian","familyName":"Jaszczur","name":"Jaszczur, Sebastian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marek","familyName":"Cygan","name":"Cygan, Marek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kamil","familyName":"Adamczewski","name":"Adamczewski, Kamil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan","familyName":"Ludziejewski","name":"Ludziejewski, Jan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Projected Compression: Trainable Projection for Efficient Transformer Compression"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T14:24:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:40:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models have steadily increased in size to achieve improved performance; however, this growth has also led to greater inference time and computational demands. Consequently, there is rising interest in model size reduction methods. To address this issue, we propose Projected Compression, a novel model compression technique, that reduces model weights by utilizing projection modules. Specifically, we first train additional trainable projections weights and preserve access to all the original model parameters. Subsequently, these projections are merged into a lower-dimensional product matrix, resulting in a reduced-size standard Transformer-based model. Unlike alternative approaches that require additional computational overhead, our method matches the base model's per-token computation step in FLOPs. Experimental results show that Projected Compression outperforms the comparable hard pruning and retraining approach on higher quality models. Moreover, the performance margin scales well with the number of tokens."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22255","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:37:59Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:37:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:37:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22210","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22210","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22210","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weronika","familyName":"Łajewska","name":"Łajewska, Weronika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ivica","familyName":"Kostric","name":"Kostric, Ivica","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gabriel","familyName":"Iturra-Bocaz","name":"Iturra-Bocaz, Gabriel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mariam","familyName":"Arustashvili","name":"Arustashvili, Mariam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Krisztian","familyName":"Balog","name":"Balog, Krisztian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"UiS-IAI@LiveRAG: Retrieval-Augmented Information Nugget-Based Generation of Responses"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T13:29:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:38:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) faces challenges related to factual correctness, source attribution, and response completeness. The LiveRAG Challenge hosted at SIGIR'25 aims to advance RAG research using a fixed corpus and a shared, open-source LLM. We propose a modular pipeline that operates on information nuggets-minimal, atomic units of relevant information extracted from retrieved documents. This multistage pipeline encompasses query rewriting, passage retrieval and reranking, nugget detection and clustering, cluster ranking and summarization, and response fluency enhancement. This design inherently promotes grounding in specific facts, facilitates source attribution, and ensures maximum information inclusion within length constraints. In this challenge, we extend our focus to also address the retrieval component of RAG, building upon our prior work on multi-faceted query rewriting. Furthermore, for augmented generation, we concentrate on improving context curation capabilities, maximizing the breadth of information covered in the response while ensuring pipeline efficiency. Our results show that combining original queries with a few sub-query rewrites boosts recall, while increasing the number of documents used for reranking and generation beyond a certain point reduces effectiveness, without improving response quality."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22210","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:36:55Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:36:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:36:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22185","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22185","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22185","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matteo","familyName":"Esposito","name":"Esposito, Matteo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Bakhtin","name":"Bakhtin, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Noman","familyName":"Ahmad","name":"Ahmad, Noman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mikel","familyName":"Robredo","name":"Robredo, Mikel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruoyu","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Ruoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Valentina","familyName":"Lenarduzzi","name":"Lenarduzzi, Valentina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Davide","familyName":"Taibi","name":"Taibi, Davide","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Autonomic Microservice Management via Agentic AI and MAPE-K Integration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T12:46:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:36:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While microservices are revolutionizing cloud computing by offering unparalleled scalability and independent deployment, their decentralized nature poses significant security and management challenges that can threaten system stability. We propose a framework based on MAPE-K, which leverages agentic AI, for autonomous anomaly detection and remediation to address the daunting task of highly distributed system management. Our framework offers practical, industry-ready solutions for maintaining robust and secure microservices. Practitioners and researchers can customize the framework to enhance system stability, reduce downtime, and monitor broader system quality attributes such as system performance level, resilience, security, and anomaly management, among others."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22185","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:36:20Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:36:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:36:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22161","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22161","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22161","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taijin","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Taijin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heqian","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Heqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lanxiao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Lanxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fanman","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Fanman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingbo","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Qingbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongliang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hongliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Attention-disentangled Uniform Orthogonal Feature Space Optimization for Few-shot Object Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T12:17:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:35:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Few-shot object detection (FSOD) aims to detect objects with limited samples for novel classes, while relying on abundant data for base classes. Existing FSOD approaches, predominantly built on the Faster R-CNN detector, entangle objectness recognition and foreground classification within shared feature spaces. This paradigm inherently establishes class-specific objectness criteria and suffers from unrepresentative novel class samples. To resolve this limitation, we propose a Uniform Orthogonal Feature Space (UOFS) optimization framework. First, UOFS decouples the feature space into two orthogonal components, where magnitude encodes objectness and angle encodes classification. This decoupling enables transferring class-agnostic objectness knowledge from base classes to novel classes. Moreover, implementing the disentanglement requires careful attention to two challenges: (1) Base set images contain unlabeled foreground instances, causing confusion between potential novel class instances and backgrounds. (2) Angular optimization depends exclusively on base class foreground instances, inducing overfitting of angular distributions to base classes. To address these challenges, we propose a Hybrid Background Optimization (HBO) strategy: (1) Constructing a pure background base set by removing unlabeled instances in original images to provide unbiased magnitude-based objectness supervision. (2) Incorporating unlabeled foreground instances in the original base set into angular optimization to enhance distribution uniformity. Additionally, we propose a Spatial-wise Attention Disentanglement and Association (SADA) module to address task conflicts between class-agnostic and class-specific tasks. Experiments demonstrate that our method significantly outperforms existing approaches based on entangled feature spaces."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22161","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:35:46Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:35:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:35:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22157","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22157","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22157","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianshu","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Tianshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Xiang","name":"Xiang, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingchuan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Mingchuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pei","familyName":"Ke","name":"Ke, Pei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bosi","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Bosi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cunxiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Cunxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiale","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Jiale","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Mu","name":"Mu, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuxiong","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Chuxiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minlie","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Minlie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Training Language Model to Critique for Better Refinement"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T12:10:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:34:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable evaluation and critique capabilities, providing insightful feedback and identifying flaws in various tasks. However, limited research has explored which types of critiques are most effective for improving model responses or how to generate such critiques. To address this gap, we introduce \\textbf{R}efinement-oriented \\textbf{C}ritique \\textbf{O}ptimization (RCO), a novel framework designed to train critic models using refinement signals. RCO uses a feedback loop where critiques, generated by the critic model, guide the actor model in refining its responses. The critique utility (CU) quantifies the effectiveness of these refinements, serving as the reward signal for training the critic model. By focusing on critiques that lead to better refinements, RCO eliminates the need for direct critique preference assessment, ensuring that critiques driving meaningful improvements are rewarded. We evaluate RCO across five tasks, i.e., dialog generation, summarization, question answering, mathematical reasoning, and code generation, and show that it significantly outperforms traditional methods and open-source models in terms of critique quality and refinement outcomes. Our contributions include the introduction of RCO, a novel supervision scheme based on refined response preferences, and comprehensive experimental results that highlight the method's effectiveness in enhancing LLM critique-refinement loops."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ACL 2025 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22157","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:35:40Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:35:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:35:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22084","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22084","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22084","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaitanya K.","familyName":"Joshi","name":"Joshi, Chaitanya K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Transformers are Graph Neural Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T10:15:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:31:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We establish connections between the Transformer architecture, originally introduced for natural language processing, and Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) for representation learning on graphs. We show how Transformers can be viewed as message passing GNNs operating on fully connected graphs of tokens, where the self-attention mechanism capture the relative importance of all tokens w.r.t. each-other, and positional encodings provide hints about sequential ordering or structure. Thus, Transformers are expressive set processing networks that learn relationships among input elements without being constrained by apriori graphs. Despite this mathematical connection to GNNs, Transformers are implemented via dense matrix operations that are significantly more efficient on modern hardware than sparse message passing. This leads to the perspective that Transformers are GNNs currently winning the hardware lottery."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper is a technical version of an article in The Gradient at https://thegradient.pub/transformers-are-graph-neural-networks/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22084","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:33:56Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:33:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:33:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22056","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22056","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22056","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyan","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Ziyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifei","familyName":"Leng","name":"Leng, Yifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenbang","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Zhenbang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zora Zhiruo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zora Zhiruo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanyi","familyName":"Shang","name":"Shang, Yanyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dehan","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Dehan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Universal Retrieval for Multimodal Trajectory Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T09:50:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:29:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Trajectory data, capturing human actions and environmental states across various modalities, holds significant potential for enhancing AI agent capabilities, particularly in GUI environments. However, how to model the representation of trajectory-level data presents a significant challenge that has not been systematically addressed amid explosive trajectory data growth. In this work, we introduce Multimodal Trajectory Retrieval, bridging the gap between universal retrieval and agent-centric trajectory modeling. We construct the Unified Agent Trajectory Dataset (UATD) from annotated demonstrations and states across diverse real-world scenarios. Based on this, we present GAE-Bench, a benchmark containing a large number of trajectory-based retrieval pairs. In addition, we propose GAE-Retriever, a multimodal retrieval framework that adopts vision-language models and incorporates optimized contrastive learning through a token selection and the GradCache mechanism. Comprehensive evaluations across multiple datasets show that GAE-Retriever consistently outperforms strong baselines in retrieval recall, highlighting its effectiveness in advancing multimodal trajectory retrieval."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 3 figures, accepted by Workshop on Computer-use Agents @ ICML 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22056","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:33:17Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:33:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:33:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22033","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22033","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22033","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongchao","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yongchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bohan","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Bohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SiPipe: Bridging the CPU-GPU Utilization Gap for Efficient Pipeline-Parallel LLM Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T09:27:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:28:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As inference workloads for large language models (LLMs) scale to meet growing user demand, pipeline parallelism (PP) has become a widely adopted strategy for multi-GPU deployment, particularly in cross-node setups, to improve key-value (KV) cache capacity and inference throughput. However, PP suffers from inherent inefficiencies caused by three types of execution bubbles-load-imbalance, intra-stage, and inter-stage-which limit pipeline saturation. We present SiPipe, a heterogeneous pipeline design that improves throughput by leveraging underutilized CPU resources to offload auxiliary computation and communication. SiPipe incorporates three key techniques-CPU sampling, a token-safe execution model, and structure-aware transmission-to mitigate pipeline bubbles and improve execution efficiency. Across diverse LLMs, SiPipe achieves up to 2.1 times higher throughput, 43% lower per-token latency, and up to 23% higher average GPU utilization compared to the state-of-the-art vLLM under the same PP configuration, demonstrating its generality across LLMs and deployment scenarios."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22033","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:32:45Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:32:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:32:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22008","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22008","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22008","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessandro","familyName":"Sestini","name":"Sestini, Alessandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joakim","familyName":"Bergdahl","name":"Bergdahl, Joakim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Konrad","familyName":"Tollmar","name":"Tollmar, Konrad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew D.","familyName":"Bagdanov","name":"Bagdanov, Andrew D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linus","familyName":"Gisslén","name":"Gisslén, Linus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TROFI: Trajectory-Ranked Offline Inverse Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T08:22:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:26:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In offline reinforcement learning, agents are trained using only a fixed set of stored transitions derived from a source policy. However, this requires that the dataset be labeled by a reward function. In applied settings such as video game development, the availability of the reward function is not always guaranteed. This paper proposes Trajectory-Ranked OFfline Inverse reinforcement learning (TROFI), a novel approach to effectively learn a policy offline without a pre-defined reward function. TROFI first learns a reward function from human preferences, which it then uses to label the original dataset making it usable for training the policy. In contrast to other approaches, our method does not require optimal trajectories. Through experiments on the D4RL benchmark we demonstrate that TROFI consistently outperforms baselines and performs comparably to using the ground truth reward to learn policies. Additionally, we validate the efficacy of our method in a 3D game environment. Our studies of the reward model highlight the importance of the reward function in this setting: we show that to ensure the alignment of a value function to the actual future discounted reward, it is fundamental to have a well-engineered and easy-to-learn reward function."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published at Reinforcement Learning and Video Games Workshop at RLC 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22008","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:32:10Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:32:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:32:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22007","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22007","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22007","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liudi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Liudi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"George","familyName":"Eskandar","name":"Eskandar, George","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fengyi","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Fengyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad","familyName":"Altillawi","name":"Altillawi, Mohammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soumajit","familyName":"Majumder","name":"Majumder, Soumajit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ziyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gitta","familyName":"Kutyniok","name":"Kutyniok, Gitta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhinav","familyName":"Valada","name":"Valada, Abhinav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RoboEnvision: A Long-Horizon Video Generation Model for Multi-Task Robot Manipulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T08:21:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:26:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We address the problem of generating long-horizon videos for robotic manipulation tasks. Text-to-video diffusion models have made significant progress in photorealism, language understanding, and motion generation but struggle with long-horizon robotic tasks. Recent works use video diffusion models for high-quality simulation data and predictive rollouts in robot planning. However, these works predict short sequences of the robot achieving one task and employ an autoregressive paradigm to extend to the long horizon, leading to error accumulations in the generated video and in the execution. To overcome these limitations, we propose a novel pipeline that bypasses the need for autoregressive generation. We achieve this through a threefold contribution: 1) we first decompose the high-level goals into smaller atomic tasks and generate keyframes aligned with these instructions. A second diffusion model then interpolates between each of the two generated frames, achieving the long-horizon video. 2) We propose a semantics preserving attention module to maintain consistency between the keyframes. 3) We design a lightweight policy model to regress the robot joint states from generated videos. Our approach achieves state-of-the-art results on two benchmarks in video quality and consistency while outperforming previous policy models on long-horizon tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22007","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:32:08Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:32:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:32:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21976","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21976","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21976","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuhan","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Shuhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John","familyName":"Lambert","name":"Lambert, John","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong","familyName":"Jeon","name":"Jeon, Hong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sakshum","familyName":"Kulshrestha","name":"Kulshrestha, Sakshum","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijing","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Yijing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dragomir","familyName":"Anguelov","name":"Anguelov, Dragomir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingxing","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Mingxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chiyu Max","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Chiyu Max","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SceneDiffuser++: City-Scale Traffic Simulation via a Generative World Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T07:35:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:23:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The goal of traffic simulation is to augment a potentially limited amount of manually-driven miles that is available for testing and validation, with a much larger amount of simulated synthetic miles. The culmination of this vision would be a generative simulated city, where given a map of the city and an autonomous vehicle (AV) software stack, the simulator can seamlessly simulate the trip from point A to point B by populating the city around the AV and controlling all aspects of the scene, from animating the dynamic agents (e.g., vehicles, pedestrians) to controlling the traffic light states. We refer to this vision as CitySim, which requires an agglomeration of simulation technologies: scene generation to populate the initial scene, agent behavior modeling to animate the scene, occlusion reasoning, dynamic scene generation to seamlessly spawn and remove agents, and environment simulation for factors such as traffic lights. While some key technologies have been separately studied in various works, others such as dynamic scene generation and environment simulation have received less attention in the research community. We propose SceneDiffuser++, the first end-to-end generative world model trained on a single loss function capable of point A-to-B simulation on a city scale integrating all the requirements above. We demonstrate the city-scale traffic simulation capability of SceneDiffuser++ and study its superior realism under long simulation conditions. We evaluate the simulation quality on an augmented version of the Waymo Open Motion Dataset (WOMD) with larger map regions to support trip-level simulation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to CVPR 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21976","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:31:24Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:31:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:31:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21967","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21967","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21967","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weimin","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Weimin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ke","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanchao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hanchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sai","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Sai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sujian","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Sujian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"More Vulnerable than You Think: On the Stability of Tool-Integrated LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T07:13:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:22:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current evaluations of tool-integrated LLM agents typically focus on end-to-end tool-usage evaluation while neglecting their stability. This limits their real-world applicability, as various internal or external factors can cause agents to crash or behave abnormally. Our research addresses this by investigating whether agents are vulnerable to errors throughout the entire tool invocation process, including reading tool documentation, selecting tools and generating parameters, and processing the tool's response. Through extensive experiments, we observe that agents are highly susceptible to errors at each stage and agents based on open-source models are more vulnerable than those based on proprietary models. We also find that increasing the model size does not significantly improve tool invocation reasoning and may make agents more vulnerable to attacks resembling normal user instructions. This highlights the importance of evaluating agent stability and offers valuable insights for future LLM development and evaluation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21967","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:31:12Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:31:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:31:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21964","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21964","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21964","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael A.","familyName":"Riegler","name":"Riegler, Michael A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kristoffer Herland","familyName":"Hellton","name":"Hellton, Kristoffer Herland","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vajira","familyName":"Thambawita","name":"Thambawita, Vajira","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hugo L.","familyName":"Hammer","name":"Hammer, Hugo L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Using Large Language Models to Suggest Informative Prior Distributions in Bayesian Statistics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T07:11:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:22:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Selecting prior distributions in Bayesian statistics is challenging, resource-intensive, and subjective. We analyze using large-language models (LLMs) to suggest suitable, knowledge-based informative priors. We developed an extensive prompt asking LLMs not only to suggest priors but also to verify and reflect on their choices.\n We evaluated Claude Opus, Gemini 2.5 Pro, and ChatGPT-4o-mini on two real datasets: heart disease risk and concrete strength. All LLMs correctly identified the direction for all associations (e.g., that heart disease risk is higher for males). The quality of suggested priors was measured by their Kullback-Leibler divergence from the maximum likelihood estimator's distribution.\n The LLMs suggested both moderately and weakly informative priors. The moderate priors were often overconfident, resulting in distributions misaligned with the data. In our experiments, Claude and Gemini provided better priors than ChatGPT. For weakly informative priors, a key performance difference emerged: ChatGPT and Gemini defaulted to an \"unnecessarily vague\" mean of 0, while Claude did not, demonstrating a significant advantage.\n The ability of LLMs to identify correct associations shows their great potential as an efficient, objective method for developing informative priors. However, the primary challenge remains in calibrating the width of these priors to avoid over- and under-confidence."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21964","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:31:07Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:31:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:31:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21934","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21934","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21934","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Najmeh","familyName":"Forouzandehmehr","name":"Forouzandehmehr, Najmeh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reza Yousefi","familyName":"Maragheh","name":"Maragheh, Reza Yousefi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sriram","familyName":"Kollipara","name":"Kollipara, Sriram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Topojoy","familyName":"Biswas","name":"Biswas, Topojoy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Evren","familyName":"Korpeoglu","name":"Korpeoglu, Evren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kannan","familyName":"Achan","name":"Achan, Kannan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CAL-RAG: Retrieval-Augmented Multi-Agent Generation for Content-Aware Layout Design"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.3.3; I.2.11; H.5.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T06:09:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:20:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automated content-aware layout generation -- the task of arranging visual elements such as text, logos, and underlays on a background canvas -- remains a fundamental yet under-explored problem in intelligent design systems. While recent advances in deep generative models and large language models (LLMs) have shown promise in structured content generation, most existing approaches lack grounding in contextual design exemplars and fall short in handling semantic alignment and visual coherence. In this work we introduce CAL-RAG, a retrieval-augmented, agentic framework for content-aware layout generation that integrates multimodal retrieval, large language models, and collaborative agentic reasoning. Our system retrieves relevant layout examples from a structured knowledge base and invokes an LLM-based layout recommender to propose structured element placements. A vision-language grader agent evaluates the layout with visual metrics, and a feedback agent provides targeted refinements, enabling iterative improvement. We implement our framework using LangGraph and evaluate it on the PKU PosterLayout dataset, a benchmark rich in semantic and structural variability. CAL-RAG achieves state-of-the-art performance across multiple layout metrics -- including underlay effectiveness, element alignment, and overlap -- substantially outperforming strong baselines such as LayoutPrompter. These results demonstrate that combining retrieval augmentation with agentic multi-step reasoning yields a scalable, interpretable, and high-fidelity solution for automated layout generation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21934","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:30:25Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:30:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:30:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21924","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21924","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21924","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhao","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Zhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rong-Cheng","familyName":"Tu","name":"Tu, Rong-Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyi","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Jingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Wenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shunyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shunyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dacheng","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Dacheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SPAZER: Spatial-Semantic Progressive Reasoning Agent for Zero-shot 3D Visual Grounding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T05:34:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:19:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"3D Visual Grounding (3DVG) aims to localize target objects within a 3D scene based on natural language queries. To alleviate the reliance on costly 3D training data, recent studies have explored zero-shot 3DVG by leveraging the extensive knowledge and powerful reasoning capabilities of pre-trained LLMs and VLMs. However, existing paradigms tend to emphasize either spatial (3D-based) or semantic (2D-based) understanding, limiting their effectiveness in complex real-world applications. In this work, we introduce SPAZER - a VLM-driven agent that combines both modalities in a progressive reasoning framework. It first holistically analyzes the scene and produces a 3D rendering from the optimal viewpoint. Based on this, anchor-guided candidate screening is conducted to perform a coarse-level localization of potential objects. Furthermore, leveraging retrieved relevant 2D camera images, 3D-2D joint decision-making is efficiently performed to determine the best-matching object. By bridging spatial and semantic reasoning neural streams, SPAZER achieves robust zero-shot grounding without training on 3D-labeled data. Extensive experiments on ScanRefer and Nr3D benchmarks demonstrate that SPAZER significantly outperforms previous state-of-the-art zero-shot methods, achieving notable gains of 9.0% and 10.9% in accuracy."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21924","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:30:11Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:30:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:30:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":38,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?query=doi%3A%28%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21924%22+OR+%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21934%22+OR+%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21964%22+OR+%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21967%22+OR+%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21976%22+OR+%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.22007%22+OR+%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.22008%22+O